1
00:00:01,000 --> 00:00:04,000
Welcome back to the VentureX tutorial series.

2
00:00:04,200 --> 00:00:08,500
In this video, we're tackling Inventory
and Procurement —

3
00:00:08,600 --> 00:00:11,500
how to manage your products, track
stock levels, handle purchase orders,

4
00:00:11,600 --> 00:00:15,000
and keep your supply chain
running smoothly. Let's get into it.

5
00:00:15,300 --> 00:00:19,000
The Inventory module in VentureX is built
around five key areas:

6
00:00:19,100 --> 00:00:22,500
Products — your entire catalog,
Categories — how you organize products,

7
00:00:22,600 --> 00:00:26,000
Stock Levels — real-time inventory
quantities, Transfers — moving stock

8
00:00:26,100 --> 00:00:29,500
between locations, and Adjustments —
correcting counts when reality doesn't

9
00:00:29,600 --> 00:00:33,000
match the system. Let's start
with categories.

10
00:00:33,300 --> 00:00:37,000
Categories help you organize your
products logically. Let's create one.

11
00:00:37,100 --> 00:00:40,500
I'll click Add Category and name it
'Software Licenses'.

12
00:00:40,600 --> 00:00:44,000
Parent category can be left blank
for top-level, or you can create

13
00:00:44,100 --> 00:00:47,500
hierarchies like Electronics >
Laptops > Gaming.

14
00:00:47,600 --> 00:00:50,500
Let's keep it simple and save.

15
00:00:50,800 --> 00:00:54,000
Now let's add a product.
Navigate to Products and click Add Product.

16
00:00:54,300 --> 00:00:58,000
Product name: 'VentureX Pro —
Annual License'. SKU: VNTX-PRO-001 —

17
00:00:58,100 --> 00:01:01,500
VentureX auto-generates SKUs
if you prefer, but I like being explicit.

18
00:01:01,600 --> 00:01:05,000
Category: Software Licenses.
Description: full product description

19
00:01:05,100 --> 00:01:08,000
for internal reference.
Product type — is this a physical product

20
00:01:08,100 --> 00:01:11,500
you ship, a digital product you deliver,
or a service? I'll choose Digital

21
00:01:11,600 --> 00:01:14,500
since this is a software license.

22
00:01:14,800 --> 00:01:18,500
Pricing — let's set the regular price
at $2,400 per year. Cost price —

23
00:01:18,600 --> 00:01:22,000
what it costs you to deliver — let's
say $0 for digital.

24
00:01:22,100 --> 00:01:25,500
Tax class: Standard.
The selling price field lets you set

25
00:01:25,600 --> 00:01:28,500
promotional or tiered pricing.
I'll leave it at the regular price for now.

26
00:01:28,800 --> 00:01:32,500
Inventory tracking — toggle this on
if you want VentureX to manage

27
00:01:32,600 --> 00:01:36,000
stock quantities. For digital products,
you'd typically set this to unlimited

28
00:01:36,100 --> 00:01:39,000
or high stock. Let me enable it and
set stock quantity to 9999.

29
00:01:39,100 --> 00:01:42,500
Low stock threshold: 100 —
we'll get an alert when we dip below that.

30
00:01:42,600 --> 00:01:46,000
Manage stock by location: enabled
if you have multiple warehouses.

31
00:01:46,100 --> 00:01:49,500
I'll keep it simple with one location
for now.

32
00:01:49,800 --> 00:01:53,000
Weight and dimensions — optional for
digital products but required

33
00:01:53,100 --> 00:01:56,500
if you're shipping physical goods.
Product images — you can upload multiple

34
00:01:56,600 --> 00:02:00,000
images that appear in the catalog.
Let me save this product.

35
00:02:00,300 --> 00:02:04,000
There's our product in the list with a
stock quantity of 9999.

36
00:02:04,100 --> 00:02:07,500
Let's add a physical product too.
I'll quickly create 'USB-C Docking Station'

37
00:02:07,600 --> 00:02:11,000
as a physical product with a stock
of 50 units.

38
00:02:11,300 --> 00:02:15,000
The Stock Levels page gives you a
real-time view of every product's

39
00:02:15,100 --> 00:02:18,500
inventory status. You can see
current stock, reserved stock —

40
00:02:18,600 --> 00:02:22,000
that's items in pending orders —
available stock, and the low stock flag.

41
00:02:22,100 --> 00:02:25,000
Let me show you what happens
when we sell something.

42
00:02:25,300 --> 00:02:29,000
Stock Adjustments are for correcting
discrepancies. Maybe you did a physical

43
00:02:29,100 --> 00:02:32,500
count and found 48 docking stations
instead of 50. Let's log an adjustment.

44
00:02:32,800 --> 00:02:36,500
Product: USB-C Docking Station.
Adjustment type: Decrease. Quantity: 2.

45
00:02:36,600 --> 00:02:40,000
Reason: 'Physical count variance —
2 units unaccounted for'.

46
00:02:40,100 --> 00:02:43,500
Reference: 'Count 2026-08-20'.
This keeps a full audit trail

47
00:02:43,600 --> 00:02:46,500
of every stock change.

48
00:02:46,800 --> 00:02:50,000
Stock is now at 48 units.
Every adjustment is logged with

49
00:02:50,100 --> 00:02:53,500
who made it, when, and why —
full traceability.

50
00:02:53,800 --> 00:02:57,500
If you operate multiple locations
or warehouses, Stock Transfers let you

51
00:02:57,600 --> 00:03:01,000
move inventory between them.
Let's say we have a main warehouse

52
00:03:01,100 --> 00:03:04,000
and a satellite office.
I'll create a transfer.

53
00:03:04,300 --> 00:03:08,000
From Location: Main Warehouse.
To Location: Satellite Office.

54
00:03:08,100 --> 00:03:11,500
Product: USB-C Docking Station.
Quantity: 5. We're shipping 5 docking

55
00:03:11,600 --> 00:03:15,000
stations to the satellite office.
Add a note: 'Restocking satellite office

56
00:03:15,100 --> 00:03:18,000
for local fulfillment.'

57
00:03:18,100 --> 00:03:21,500
The transfer shows as pending
until received. When the satellite office

58
00:03:21,600 --> 00:03:25,000
confirms receipt, they mark it complete,
and stock is deducted from the main

59
00:03:25,100 --> 00:03:28,000
warehouse and added to the satellite.
Full audit trail, zero guesswork.

60
00:03:28,300 --> 00:03:32,000
Remember our low stock threshold of 10
for the docking station? When available

61
00:03:32,100 --> 00:03:35,500
stock drops below that number,
VentureX triggers a low stock alert.

62
00:03:35,600 --> 00:03:39,000
Let me simulate this by adjusting
stock to 8 units.

63
00:03:39,300 --> 00:03:43,000
There it is — a low stock alert badge
appeared in the Inventory module

64
00:03:43,100 --> 00:03:46,500
and in the notification center.
Users with the right permissions

65
00:03:46,600 --> 00:03:50,000
get emailed alerts too.
You can configure alert thresholds

66
00:03:50,100 --> 00:03:53,000
per product and per user role
in Settings.

67
00:03:53,300 --> 00:03:57,000
Now let's flip to the other side of
the coin: Procurement. This module

68
00:03:57,100 --> 00:04:00,500
handles purchasing from your suppliers —
creating purchase orders, receiving goods,

69
00:04:00,600 --> 00:04:03,500
and managing vendor relationships.

70
00:04:03,800 --> 00:04:07,500
First, let's add a vendor.
Navigate to Vendors and click Add Vendor.

71
00:04:07,800 --> 00:04:11,500
Vendor name: 'TechSupply Global'.
Contact person: 'Mike Chen'.

72
00:04:11,600 --> 00:04:15,000
Email: purchasing@techsupply-global.com.
Phone: 555-0300.

73
00:04:15,100 --> 00:04:18,500
Address: their shipping address.
Payment terms: Net 30.

74
00:04:18,600 --> 00:04:22,000
Tax ID: optional but useful for
accounting. I'll also add the tag

75
00:04:22,100 --> 00:04:25,000
'Preferred Vendor'. Save.

76
00:04:25,300 --> 00:04:29,000
Now let's create a purchase order.
Navigate to Purchase Orders

77
00:04:29,100 --> 00:04:32,000
and click Create PO.

78
00:04:32,300 --> 00:04:36,000
Vendor: TechSupply Global.
PO number auto-generates but I'll set it

79
00:04:36,100 --> 00:04:39,500
manually to PO-2026-001.
Order date: today.

80
00:04:39,600 --> 00:04:43,000
Expected delivery: two weeks out.
Now the line items —

81
00:04:43,100 --> 00:04:46,500
I'm ordering 100 USB-C Docking Stations
at $95 each, that's our cost price.

82
00:04:46,600 --> 00:04:50,000
Add another line: 50 units of a
Wireless Mouse at $22 each.

83
00:04:50,300 --> 00:04:54,000
The total updates automatically —
$9,500 plus $1,100, totaling $10,600.

84
00:04:54,100 --> 00:04:57,500
You can add notes, attach files,
and set approval workflows

85
00:04:57,600 --> 00:05:00,500
if your organization requires PO approval
before sending to vendors.

86
00:05:00,800 --> 00:05:04,500
The PO is saved. Status is Pending.
When you send it to the vendor,

87
00:05:04,600 --> 00:05:08,000
you change it to Sent.
When goods arrive, you receive them.

88
00:05:08,300 --> 00:05:12,000
Let's simulate receiving this order.
I'll open the PO and click Receive Items.

89
00:05:12,300 --> 00:05:16,000
The receiving screen shows each line item.
I ordered 100 docking stations — all 100

90
00:05:16,100 --> 00:05:19,500
arrived in good condition, so I'll
confirm 100 received.

91
00:05:19,600 --> 00:05:23,000
Wireless mice — let's say 48 arrived,
2 were damaged in transit. I'll enter 48

92
00:05:23,100 --> 00:05:26,500
as received and note the discrepancy.
When I save this, stock levels update

93
00:05:26,600 --> 00:05:30,000
automatically — 100 docking stations
and 48 mice are added to inventory.

94
00:05:30,300 --> 00:05:34,000
Check the Stock Levels page — our
docking station stock is now at 148,

95
00:05:34,100 --> 00:05:37,500
and we have 48 wireless mice.
The PO status changes to Partially Received

96
00:05:37,600 --> 00:05:41,000
or Fully Received depending on what
came in. If there's a discrepancy

97
00:05:41,100 --> 00:05:44,500
with the vendor, you can log it
and track the resolution.

98
00:05:44,800 --> 00:05:48,500
VentureX gives you solid inventory
reporting. You can view stock valuation —

99
00:05:48,600 --> 00:05:52,000
what your inventory is worth at cost
and at retail. Stock movement history —

100
00:05:52,100 --> 00:05:55,500
every adjustment, transfer, and receipt
logged chronologically.

101
00:05:55,600 --> 00:05:59,000
Low stock reports — which products
need reordering. Purchase order history —

102
00:05:59,100 --> 00:06:02,500
what you've ordered, from whom, and when.
And supplier performance —

103
00:06:02,600 --> 00:06:05,500
lead times, fill rates,
and defect rates.

104
00:06:05,800 --> 00:06:09,500
Our stock valuation shows $18,915 in
inventory at cost — 148 docking stations

105
00:06:09,600 --> 00:06:13,000
at $95, 48 mice at $22, and 9999
software licenses at $0 cost.

106
00:06:13,100 --> 00:06:16,500
This feeds directly into your accounting
module for balance sheet reporting.

107
00:06:16,800 --> 00:06:20,500
That's Inventory and Procurement
in VentureX.

108
00:06:20,600 --> 00:06:24,000
You've seen how to manage products,
categories, stock levels, adjustments,

109
00:06:24,100 --> 00:06:27,500
transfers, purchase orders, and
vendor relationships.

110
00:06:27,600 --> 00:06:31,000
In the next video, we'll explore the
AI features and how VentureX routes

111
00:06:31,100 --> 00:06:34,500
tasks across multiple AI providers.
See you there.
